How do the stages of life shape our understanding of time and mortality?
[The Start of Assistant 1’s Answer]
The stages of life shape our understanding of time and mortality in several ways. As we move through different stages, such as childhood, adolescence, adulthood, and old age, our perception of time and mortality changes. For example, as children, we may view time as an endless expanse, while as adults, we become more aware of the finite nature of time. Additionally, our understanding of mortality is shaped by the experiences we have during each stage of life. For instance, as children, we may not fully comprehend the concept of death, while as adults, we become more aware of our own mortality and the mortality of those around us. Overall, the stages of life play a significant role in shaping our understanding of time and mortality
